MODERN AWS APPROACH — IAM IDENTITY CENTER / SSO → EKS ACCESS

REAL-TIME EXAMPLE

Requirement:
John needs permission to deploy applications to the "dev" namespace
of an EKS cluster.

Flow:

John
↓
IAM Identity Center / SSO
↓
Permission Set
↓
AWSReservedSSO IAM Role
↓
EKS Access Entry
↓
EKS Access Policy
↓
dev namespace
↓
Deploy application


STEP 1 — CREATE / USE USER IN IAM IDENTITY CENTER

AWS Console
→ IAM Identity Center
→ Users
→ Create user

Example:

User:
John


STEP 2 — CREATE PERMISSION SET

AWS Console
→ IAM Identity Center
→ Permission sets
→ Create permission set

Example:

Permission Set:
EKS-Dev-Developer

Purpose:

Defines what AWS account access John gets through SSO.

IMPORTANT:

Do not create a normal IAM User for John
for this modern human-access approach.


STEP 3 — ASSIGN JOHN TO THE AWS ACCOUNT

AWS Console
→ IAM Identity Center
→ AWS accounts
→ Select the required AWS account
→ Assign users or groups
→ Select John
→ Select EKS-Dev-Developer
→ Submit

AWS creates/manages an IAM role for this SSO assignment.

Example role:

AWSReservedSSO_EKS-Dev-Developer_xxxxx


STEP 4 — GET THE SSO IAM ROLE ARN

The role will look similar to:

arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/AWSReservedSSO_EKS-Dev-Developer_xxxxx

IMPORTANT:

This IAM Role is the identity that will be given access to EKS.


STEP 5 — CREATE EKS ACCESS ENTRY

AWS Console
→ EKS
→ Clusters
→ dev-eks
→ Access
→ Create access entry

Principal ARN:

arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/AWSReservedSSO_EKS-Dev-Developer_xxxxx

IMPORTANT:

Use the IAM ROLE ARN.

Do NOT use John's IAM User ARN.


STEP 6 — ASSOCIATE EKS ACCESS POLICY

Open the newly created EKS Access Entry.

→ Add access policy

Choose the appropriate EKS Access Policy
for the required permissions.

For deployment access:

Choose an appropriate policy that allows
the required edit/write Kubernetes permissions.

Scope the access if required:

Type:
Namespace

Namespace:
dev

IMPORTANT:

Do not give cluster-admin access unless the user
actually needs cluster-wide administrative permissions.


STEP 7 — JOHN LOGS INTO AWS

John opens the company's:

AWS IAM Identity Center / AWS Access Portal

John signs in.

Then:

Select AWS Account
↓
Select the assigned role
↓
AWS provides temporary credentials


STEP 8 — CONFIGURE KUBECTL

John runs:

aws eks update-kubeconfig \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --name dev-eks


STEP 9 — TEST ACCESS

Check pods:

kubectl get pods -n dev

Check deployments:

kubectl get deployments -n dev


STEP 10 — DEPLOY APPLICATION

If the assigned EKS Access Policy allows deployment:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml -n dev

Example:

kubectl rollout restart deployment myapp -n dev

kubectl scale deployment myapp --replicas=3 -n dev


FINAL FLOW:

John
↓
IAM Identity Center / SSO
↓
Permission Set
↓
AWSReservedSSO IAM Role
↓
EKS Access Entry
↓
EKS Access Policy
↓
dev namespace
↓
Kubernetes resources
↓
Deploy application


WHAT EACH PART DOES:

IAM Identity Center / SSO
→ Authenticates John

Permission Set
→ Defines John's AWS account access through SSO

AWSReservedSSO IAM Role
→ IAM identity used when John accesses the AWS account

EKS Access Entry
→ Connects the IAM Role to the EKS cluster

EKS Access Policy
→ Defines what the IAM Role can do inside EKS

Namespace scope
→ Limits access to the required Kubernetes namespace


IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE FROM THE OLD APPROACH:

OLD APPROACH:

IAM User
↓
sts:AssumeRole
↓
IAM Role
↓
EKS Access

MODERN APPROACH:

IAM Identity Center / SSO
↓
IAM Role
↓
EKS Access Entry
↓
EKS Access Policy


GOLDEN RULE:

For normal human AWS access:

IAM Identity Center / SSO
→ IAM Role
→ AWS permissions


For EKS:

IAM Identity Center / SSO
→ IAM Role
→ EKS Access Entry
→ EKS Access Policy
→ Kubernetes resources


ONE-LINE INTERVIEW ANSWER:

"In the modern approach, we use IAM Identity Center for human authentication, assign the user a permission set that provides an IAM role,
and then add that IAM role to the EKS Access Entry. We associate the required EKS Access Policy and 
scope it to the required namespace so the user gets only the Kubernetes permissions they need."
